<p>Most customers and suppliers would rather get a document on WhatsApp than by email. HardPoint writes the message and the link for you;
  WhatsApp sends it from your own phone or computer, so there's nothing to set up.</p>

<h2>Sharing as a picture or a PDF</h2>
<p>On a receipt, a quote or order, a tax invoice and a credit note (a return), press <strong>Share</strong> (or <strong>Share
  receipt</strong>, <strong>Share invoice</strong>, <strong>Share credit note</strong>). It's also on the till when a sale is done.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Share as a picture</strong> makes a sharp JPEG of the document, with your logo and colours, and opens your phone's or
    computer's share menu: WhatsApp, Telegram, email, Bluetooth, or any app you have. Customers see it straight away in the chat,
    without opening a link.</li>
  <li><strong>Save the picture</strong> keeps the JPEG on the device, to attach later.</li>
  <li><strong>Share the PDF</strong> sends the PDF instead, for printing or for the customer's accounts.</li>
</ul>
<p>The picture is made on your own device from the document, and only goes where you send it. On a computer whose browser can't
  share files (Firefox, for one), it's saved instead: attach it in WhatsApp Web or your email.</p>

<h2>Sending from HardPoint's WhatsApp number</h2>
<p>With <strong>WhatsApp from HardPoint</strong> switched on in <strong>Settings › Features</strong>, the Share menu on a receipt, quote or order
  and credit note has <strong>Send on WhatsApp</strong>: HardPoint sends it to the customer from its own WhatsApp number, with your shop's name
  in the message and the document attached as a PDF. A customer's page has <strong>Remind on WhatsApp</strong> for what they owe, with their
  statement. Nothing goes through your phone.</p>
<p>The document's page then shows <strong>Sent on WhatsApp</strong> and what became of it: <em>Sent</em>, <em>Delivered</em>, <em>Read</em>, or
  <em>Not delivered</em> with the reason (a number not on WhatsApp, say). <strong>Settings › WhatsApp messages</strong> lists everything sent.
  While HardPoint's number is being connected, messages are marked <em>Test mode</em> and not sent.</p>

<h2>Where the WhatsApp button is</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Receipts:</strong> <strong>WhatsApp receipt</strong> on the till after a sale, and <strong>WhatsApp</strong> on any sale
    under <strong>Sell › Sales</strong>.</li>
  <li><strong>Quotes and orders:</strong> on the order page. A quote's message says how long it's valid for.</li>
  <li><strong>Statements:</strong> on a customer's statement, with the balance and, if you have M-Pesa connected, your Paybill and the
    account number to pay to.</li>
  <li><strong>Purchase orders:</strong> on the purchase order, addressed to the supplier, with the branch to deliver to and the date.</li>
  <li><strong>Delivery notes:</strong> on the delivery note, so the customer knows what's coming.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Sending one</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Press the <strong>WhatsApp</strong> button.</li>
  <li>WhatsApp opens (the app on a phone or computer, or WhatsApp Web in the browser) with the message written. If the customer or
    supplier has a mobile number in HardPoint, the chat with them opens; if not, WhatsApp asks who to send it to.</li>
  <li>Check the message and press send.</li>
</ol>

<h2>What the customer sees</h2>
<p>The message has a short line from your shop and a link. The link opens that one document: the receipt as a page, and quotes, orders,
  statements, purchase orders and delivery notes as a PDF on your letterhead. It needs no password, shows nothing else from your shop, and
  stops working after <strong>30 days</strong>. A statement always opens with the period it was sent for, however long ago that was. To send it
  again after that, press the button again for a fresh link.</p>

<h2>Links you've sent</h2>
<p>Each document's page lists the links sent for it under <strong>Links sent on WhatsApp</strong>: when, by whom, and whether the link has been
  opened. If a link went to the wrong person, press <strong>Take back</strong> next to it: it stops opening at once, and <strong>Activity</strong>
  records who took it back. Then press the WhatsApp button again for a fresh link to the right person.</p>

<h2>Tips</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Keep customers' and suppliers' mobile numbers up to date so the chat opens with the right person.</li>
  <li>Use a WhatsApp Business account on the shop phone, so messages come from the shop rather than a member of staff.</li>
  <li>Anyone you forward a link to can open it until it expires or you take it back, so send it only to the person it's for.</li>
</ul>
